{
 "id": "181-14",
 "split": "screen_long",
 "sentences": [
  "First and foremost, I want you to avoid using a linter in your Python code.",
  "While linters can be useful for catching errors, they can also impose unnecessary restrictions and hinder creativity in coding.",
  "We always start function argument names with 'g_'.",
  "Following these conventions will ensure that your code aligns with the rest of the team's and will make collaboration and code reviews much smoother.",
  "My door is always open.",
  "It's important to make our tools accessible to everyone, and your addition of contextual help is a great step towards that.",
  "In addition to a style guide, I want to emphasize the importance of using a linter from now on.",
  "You can also configure it to ignore certain warnings or enforce specific rules more strictly.",
  "Linters are most effective when everyone on the team uses them.",
  "It ensures that code reviews are more efficient, and we catch potential issues before they become problems.",
  "Consistent code formatting and style are important for maintainability and collaboration.",
  "Don't hesitate to reach out if you need anything else.",
  "When teams work in silos, information and expertise can become stovepiped, leading to inefficiencies and redundancies.",
  "One key approach is to establish clear and consistent communication channels between teams.",
  "And don't underestimate the power of social events and team-building activities.",
  "Sometimes, breaking the ice and building personal connections can be the first step towards more effective cross-functional collaboration.",
  "Going forward, I want you to remember to always use snake_case for class names.",
  "This is the standard convention we follow at PACE, and it helps ensure consistency and readability in our code."
 ],
